<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.11.0: Lesson Overview</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01036</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.11.0: Lesson Overview</md:title>
    <md:uuid>01739dfe-e505-46ad-8d40-67e6060fd4a3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, students work to graph functions in slope-intercept form using transformations. They will identify vertical shifts, vertical stretches and compressions, and vertical rotations (reflection over the \(x\)-axis). They will also identify horizontal shifts, horizontal stretches and compressions, and horizontal rotations (reflection over the \(y\)-axis).</para>
<para>Through exploration with graphing, students will recognize patterns for the transformations and understand how the constants applied to \(f(x)\) determine the transformations needed to graph the function. Specifically, students learn about: </para>

<table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>Vertical Shifts</entry>
      <entry>\(f(x) + d\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>Horizontal Shifts</entry>
      <entry>\(f(x - c)\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>Vertical Stretches and Compressions (Dilations)</entry>
      <entry>\(af(x)\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>Horizontal Stretches and Compressions (Dilations)</entry>
      <entry>\(f(bx)\)</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Students will also be able to describe how one function transforms from the parent function, f(x)=x and how one function transforms to another.</para>
<title>Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item> Use transformations to graph linear functions. </item>
  <item> Recognize the shifts made to a parent linear function to form another linear function. </item>
</list>
<title> Learning Targets (Student Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item> Use transformations to graph linear functions. </item>
  <item> Recognize the shifts made to a parent linear function to form another linear function. </item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption>
  Texas Essential Knowledge and Skills (TEKS)
  </caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">TEKS</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Explanation of Coverage</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>A1(E) create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas </entry>
      <entry>Math process coverage: Lesson provides content that supports this TEKS. </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>A1(F) analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas </entry>
      <entry>Math process coverage: Lesson provides content that supports this TEKS. </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>A2(C) <u>write linear equations in two variables given</u> a table of values, <u>a graph</u>, and a verbal description </entry>
      <entry>Partial coverage: Lesson provides content that covers part of this TEKS. The parts that are covered have been underlined. </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>A3(E) determine the effects on the graph of the parent function
        \(f(x) = x\) when \(f(x)\) is replaced by \(af(x), f(x) + d, f(x - c), f(bx)\) for specific values of \(a, b, c\), and \(d\)</entry>
      <entry>Full coverage: Lesson provides content that covers all parts of this TEKS. </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>Lesson Activities</title>
<para>Here are the instructional activities for the lesson:</para>
<list>
  <item>4.11.1: Graphing Linear Functions</item>
  <item>4.11.2: Vertical Shifts
    <list>
      <item>4.11.2: Self-Check</item>
      <item>4.11.2: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>4.11.3: Horizonntal Shifts
    <list>
      <item>4.11.3: Self-Check</item>
      <item>4.11.3: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>4.11.4: Vertical Stretches and Compressions
    <list>
      <item>4.11.4: Self-Check</item>
      <item>4.11.4: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>4.11.5: Horizontal Stretches and Compressions
    <list>
      <item>4.11.5: Self-Check</item>
      <item>4.11.5: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>4.11.6: Graphing Using Transformations</item>
</list>
<para>Students will also complete a series of problems in the 4.11.5: Practice.</para>
<title>Required Materials</title>
<list>
  <item> coordinate grids </item>
  <item> rulers </item>
  <item> colored pencils </item>
  <item>geoboards (optional) </item>
  <item> rubber bands of different colors (optional)</item>
  <item> marshmallows (optional) </item>
</list>
<title>Required Preparation</title>
<para>None</para>
<title>Lesson Vocabulary</title>
<para>During this lesson, it is important to:</para>
<list>
  <item> Familiarize students with the vocabulary words they will see throughout the lesson. Encourage students to look for these words and notice their use and meanings. </item>
  <item> Encourage students to use key vocabulary words in “math talk” and their written and oral explanations. </item>
  <item> Utilize a word wall. Sample cards are located here: <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8ed3aa68763498713b44d3c2e537ffe61b4755f0">Mathematics Vocabulary Word Wall Cards</link> </item>
</list>
<para>Vocabulary words that are emphasized in this lesson include:</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> Previous Vocabulary </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> New Vocabulary </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><list>
          <item> linear function </item>
        </list></entry>
      <entry><list>
          <item> parent function </item>
          <item> transformation </item>
          <item> vertical shift </item>
          <item> vertical stretch </item>
          <item> vertical compression </item>
          <item> vertical reflection </item>
        </list></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para>To support newcomers or students identified at the beginning level of language proficiency, share the following Quizlet links to help students gain an understanding of the academic vocabulary. Use the Spanish versions to anchor student understanding before bridging to the English versions. </para>
<list>
  <item><link url="https://quizlet.com/881785885/raise-unit-4-spn-vocabulary-part-1-functions-graph-characteristics-flash-cards/?i=5eauv9&amp;x=1jq">Unit 4 Spanish Vocabulary, part 1</link></item>
  <item><link url="https://quizlet.com/881215410/raise-unit-4-vocabulary-part-1-functions-graph-characteristics-flash-cards/?i=5eauv9&amp;x=1jqt">Unit 4 Vocabulary, part 1</link></item>
  <item><link url="https://quizlet.com/881786064/raise-unit-4-spn-vocabulary-part-2-transformations-of-graphs-sequences-flash-cards/?i=5eauv9&amp;x=1jqt">Unit 4 Spanish Vocabulary, part 2</link></item>
  <item><link url="https://quizlet.com/881222764/raise-unit-4-vocabulary-part-2-transformations-of-graphs-sequences-flash-cards/?i=5eauv9&amp;x=1jqt">Unit 4 Vocabulary, part 2</link></item>
</list>
<title>Support for English Language Learners </title>
<para>Throughout this lesson, activities are incorporated that align to the following ELPS. The suggested activities are only a sampling of the types of support and scaffolding that can extend the learning for English language learners. Continue to find additional opportunities as you build your own set of ELL learning routines. </para>
<list>
  <item>ELPS 2(F) listen to and derive meaning from a variety of media such as audiotape, video, DVD, and CD-ROM to build and reinforce concept and language attainment </item>
  <item>ELPS 3(F) ask and give information ranging from using a very limited bank of high-frequency, high-need, concrete vocabulary, including key words and expressions needed for basic communication in academic and social contexts, to using abstract and content-based vocabulary during extended speaking assignments </item>
  <item>ELPS 4(D) use prereading supports such as graphic organizers, illustrations, and pretaught topic-related vocabulary and other prereading activities to enhance comprehension of written text </item>
</list>
<title>Support for Building Character</title>
<para>Throughout this unit, find ways to encourage and support students to work on cultivating their <emphasis>creativity</emphasis>.</para>
<para>Here are some tips to try during this lesson:</para>
<list>
  <item> <link title="Compound Interest" url="https://characterlab.org/tips-of-the-week/compound-interest/">Compound Interest</link> </item>
  <item> <link title="Free Your Mind" url="https://characterlab.org/tips-of-the-week/free-your-mind/">Free Your Mind</link> </item>
</list>
<para>You can find other tips located here in the <link url="https://characterlab.org/playbooks/creativity">Playbook on Creativity</link> from Character Lab.</para>
</section>
